L131:  | $$p\left(y\mid x_{i}^{\prime}\right)=\begin{cases}{\left[1,0,\dots,0\right]^{\top},}&\text{if }\varphi\left(f_{x^{\prime}},f_{t}\right)<\alpha\\
L132: \eta\sum_{l=1}^{k}\gamma_{k}\left(\theta\left(x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right),&\text{otherwise }\end{cases}$$  |  | (7)
L133: We apply the operator $\gamma_{k}(\cdot)$, which retains the top-k task scores while zeroing out the rest, ensuring that only the most relevant primitives contribute to clustering. $\eta$ is normalization constant. This construction highlights the most relevant task cues while suppressing noise, yielding stable $p(y\mid x^{\prime})$ for IB merges and producing clusters $\tilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}$ compact to $Q$.
L134: Cluster-Wise Grouping and Selection. To extract informative memories from a redundant pool of video captions, we leverage the task-driven clusters $\tilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}=\{\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{N}\}$ obtained previously. Our intuition is that two captions are redundant if they refer to the same cluster.
L135: Each video caption $c(T_{i})$ corresponds to the detailed scene description within the $i^{th}$ M-second segment and is associated with a set of primitive indices $I(c(T_{i}))$. To construct an informative and compact memory for contextual reasoning, we group captions by clusters $\tilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}$.
L136: Specifically, each caption $c(T_{i})\in\mathcal{C}_{Q}$ is assigned to the caption group $\mathcal{G}(\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{k})$ corresponding to cluster $\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{k}$ ($k\in\{1,\ldots,N\}$) if their index sets overlap:
L137:  | $$\mathcal{G}(\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{k})\leftarrow c(T_{i})\;\;\text{iff}\;\;I(c(T_{i}))\cap\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{k}\neq\varnothing.$$  |  | (8)
L138: 
L139: Across all clusters $\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{j}\in\tilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}$, we first select a representative caption $\hat{c}_{k}$ for each cluster and then rank all representatives globally by their semantic relevance $\phi(\hat{c}_{k},Q)$ to the query $Q$, where $\phi(\cdot,\cdot)$ measures caption-query similarity:
L140:  | $$\hat{c}_{k}=\underset{c\in\mathcal{G}(\tilde{x}^{\prime}_{k})}{\arg\max}\;\phi(c,Q),\quad k\in\{1,2,\ldots,N\}.$$  |  | (9)
L141: 
L142: Finally, we rank all representative captions based on their scores and retain the top-$K$ to form the textual memories:
L143: 
L144:  | $$R_{\text{text}}=\left\{\hat{c}_{k}\mid\mathrm{rank}\!\left(\phi(\hat{c}_{k},Q)\right)\leq K\right\}.$$  |  | (10)
L145: This process ensures that the final retrieval set $R$ is both diverse (one representative per cluster) and task-relevant (ranked by semantic importance).
L146: When the query requires finer-grained evidence beyond captions, the LLM acts as a selector that samples a set of keyframe timestamps $T^{*}=\{t_{1}^{*},t_{2}^{*},\ldots,t_{m}^{*}\}$ from the video based on the temporal information embedded in the textual memories $R_{\text{text}}$. The corresponding keyframe images are retrieved as $R_{\text{img}}(T^{*})\in\mathcal{K}_{1:T}$, and the final multimodal memory $R$ is formed by fusing the text and image modalities:
L147:  | $$R=R_{\text{text }}\oplus R_{\text{img }}\left(T^{*}\right),\text{ where }T^{*}=f_{\text{LLM}}\left(R_{\text{text }}\right)$$  |  | (11)
